      "text": "The stream that runs through Weeping Water was originally called Nigahoe, meaning 'rustling water' in Oto and Omaha.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Weeping_Water%2C_Nebraska",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "French explorers misread Nigahoe as Nihoage, translating to 'water weeping,' which became the town's English name.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Weeping_Water%2C_Nebraska",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Weeping Water was platted in 1867 and incorporated as a city by 1888.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7,
        8
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Weeping_Water%2C_Nebraska",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The town is best known for its limestone quarry, a major local industry.",
